From cb92f2596dd42cda7d93cd1f299cbb21ad463eec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Repo Review <review@example.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 21:49:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] reliability: blocking-IO isolation, proper main
 entrypoint, coordinated shutdown, self-restarting streams, pipeline metrics
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bundles the remaining review findings, all touching Main.scala and/or
the producer and consumer:

1. GoogleJwtTokenCache's Google API calls (credentials.refreshIfExpired(),
   refreshAccessToken()) are synchronous network I/O, previously run
   inside a bare Future{} with no isolation from the dispatcher backing
   Akka HTTP routes and the Kafka streams — a slow/hanging call could
   starve threads the rest of the app needs. Added a dedicated
   'blocking-io-dispatcher' (application.conf) passed explicitly to
   GoogleJwtTokenCache, plus scala.concurrent.blocking around the calls
   themselves as a second layer of protection.

2. object Main extends App used Scala's DelayedInit, meaning a
   synchronous exception during startup (before the final Await) was
   NOT caught by the existing try/catch, which only wrapped that
   Await — producing an unhandled stack trace instead of a clean
   logged exit. Replaced with an explicit def main(args) wrapping the
   entire startup path.

3. KafkaProducerWithJwt.run() / KafkaConsumerWithJwt.run() previously
   died permanently on any stream failure (a Kafka broker blip, etc.)
   with nothing to restart them. Both now wrap their pipeline in
   RestartSource.onFailuresWithBackoff (1s-30s backoff), and expose a
   shutdown() method backed by a KillSwitch so CoordinatedShutdown can
   stop them in an ORDERED way — draining in-flight sends/commits
   before the actor system's materializer goes away. The previous bare
   system.terminate() on a manual shutdown hook gave no such guarantee.
   CoordinatedShutdown registers its own JVM shutdown hook, so the old
   sys.addShutdownHook is removed as redundant.

4. Added kafka_messages_produced_total / kafka_messages_consumed_total
   Prometheus counters — previously the only pipeline observability was
   token-cache metrics and JVM stats; the actual message flow the
   project's README advertises wasn't measured at all. Produced-side
   counter reflects records handed to the sink, not confirmed broker
   acks — noted in its help text.

5. Small adjacent fix: added enable.idempotence=true to the producer,
   alongside the existing acks=all/retries=3 (turns retries from
   'might duplicate' into exactly-once per partition).

This is the largest and most structurally invasive patch of the
series — I was not able to run sbt compile / sbt test against it (no
network access in the environment I used to prepare these patches).
Please build and run this one locally before merging; in particular
double-check:
  - CoordinatedShutdown(system.toClassic) resolves correctly against
    your exact Akka version (uses the classic-system adapter, which
    should be stable across 2.6.x-2.9.x, but verify against 2.9.3).
  - RestartSource.onFailuresWithBackoff + Consumer.committableSource
    behaves as expected on an actual broker restart (kill the kafka
    container mid-run and confirm the consumer log shows a backoff
    retry rather than a dead stream).
